**Dependencies:**

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

**`application.yml`:**

```yaml
spring:
  security:
    oauth2:
      client:
        registration:
          okta:
            client-id: ${OKTA_CLIENT_ID}
            client-secret: ${OKTA_CLIENT_SECRET}
            scope: openid, profile, email
            redirect-uri: "{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}"
            client-authentication-method: client_secret_basic
        provider:
          okta:
            issuer-uri: https://example.okta.com/oauth2/default
```

**Spring auto-configures with discovery** — reads `.well-known/openid-configuration`.

**Security config:**

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SSoConfig {

    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain chain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        return http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a
                .requestMatchers("/", "/error").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated())
            .oauth2Login(oauth -> oauth
                .userInfoEndpoint(u -> u.userAuthoritiesMapper(authoritiesMapper()))
                .defaultSuccessUrl("/home", true))
            .logout(l -> l
                .logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogoutSuccessHandler())
                .invalidateHttpSession(true))
            .build();
    }

    GrantedAuthoritiesMapper authoritiesMapper() {
        return (authorities) -> {
            Set<GrantedAuthority> mapped = new HashSet<>();
            authorities.forEach(auth -> {
                if (auth instanceof OidcUserAuthority oidc) {
                    List<String> groups = oidc.getIdToken().getClaimAsStringList("groups");
                    if (groups != null)
                        groups.forEach(g -> mapped.add(
                            new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + g.toUpperCase())));
                }
            });
            return mapped;
        };
    }

    @Bean
    LogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogoutSuccessHandler() {
        OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler handler =
            new OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrationRepository);
        handler.setPostLogoutRedirectUri("{baseUrl}");
        return handler;
    }
}
```

**Accessing user info in controller:**

```java
@GetMapping("/home")
public String home(@AuthenticationPrincipal OidcUser user, Model m) {
    m.addAttribute("email", user.getEmail());
    m.addAttribute("name", user.getFullName());
    m.addAttribute("groups", user.getClaimAsStringList("groups"));
    return "home";
}
```

**PKCE highlights:**
- Automatic in Spring Security 6 with `client-authentication-method: none` (public client)
- Confidential clients: secure by default flow

**JIT provisioning (first-time login):**

```java
@Component
public class UserProvisioner implements AuthenticationSuccessHandler {
    private final UserRepository repo;

    @Override
    public void onAuthenticationSuccess(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res,
                                         Authentication auth) throws IOException {
        OidcUser user = (OidcUser) auth.getPrincipal();
        repo.findByEmail(user.getEmail())
            .orElseGet(() -> repo.save(new AppUser(
                user.getEmail(), user.getFullName(),
                parseRolesFromClaims(user))));
        res.sendRedirect("/home");
    }
}
```

**Dependencies:**

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-security-saml2-service-provider</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

**`application.yml` with metadata:**

```yaml
spring:
  security:
    saml2:
      relyingparty:
        registration:
          okta:
            identityprovider:
              entity-id: http://www.okta.com/exkxxxx
              verification:
                credentials:
                  - certificate-location: classpath:okta.cert
              singlesignon:
                url: https://example.okta.com/app/xxxx/sso/saml
                binding: POST
                sign-request: true
              singlelogout:
                url: https://example.okta.com/app/xxxx/slo/saml
                response-url: "{baseUrl}/logout/saml2/slo"
                binding: POST
            entity-id: "{baseUrl}/saml2/service-provider-metadata/{registrationId}"
            assertingparty:
              metadata-uri: https://example.okta.com/app/xxxx/sso/saml/metadata
            signing:
              credentials:
                - private-key-location: classpath:sp.key
                  certificate-location: classpath:sp.cert
```

**`metadata-uri` is the simplest** — Spring fetches IdP metadata automatically.

**Security config:**

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SamlConfig {

    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain chain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        return http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a
                .requestMatchers("/", "/error", "/saml2/**", "/login/**").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated())
            .saml2Login(saml -> saml
                .defaultSuccessUrl("/home", true))
            .saml2Logout(Customizer.withDefaults())
            .build();
    }
}
```

**Accessing user in controller:**

```java
@GetMapping("/home")
public String home(@AuthenticationPrincipal Saml2AuthenticatedPrincipal principal, Model m) {
    m.addAttribute("email", principal.getFirstAttribute("email"));
    m.addAttribute("groups", principal.getAttribute("groups"));
    return "home";
}
```

**Attribute-to-authority mapping:**

```java
@Bean
OpenSaml4AuthenticationProvider samlProvider() {
    OpenSaml4AuthenticationProvider p = new OpenSaml4AuthenticationProvider();
    p.setResponseAuthenticationConverter(responseToken -> {
        Saml2Authentication auth = OpenSaml4AuthenticationProvider
            .createDefaultResponseAuthenticationConverter().convert(responseToken);
        Saml2AuthenticatedPrincipal principal =
            (Saml2AuthenticatedPrincipal) auth.getPrincipal();
        List<String> roles = principal.getAttribute("groups");
        Collection<SimpleGrantedAuthority> authorities = roles == null ? List.of() :
            roles.stream().map(r -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + r)).toList();
        return new Saml2Authentication(principal, auth.getSaml2Response(), authorities);
    });
    return p;
}
```

**Cert rotation:** configure multiple `verification.credentials` entries; rotate by adding new, verifying, then removing old.

**SecurityFilterChain wiring:**

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SamlConfig {

    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain chain(HttpSecurity http,
                              OpenSaml4AuthenticationProvider provider) throws Exception {
        return http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a
                .requestMatchers("/", "/error", "/saml2/**", "/login/**").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated())
            .saml2Login(saml -> saml
                .authenticationManager(new ProviderManager(provider))
                .defaultSuccessUrl("/home", true))
            .saml2Logout(Customizer.withDefaults())
            .saml2Metadata(Customizer.withDefaults())
            .build();
    }

    @Bean
    OpenSaml4AuthenticationProvider samlProvider() {
        OpenSaml4AuthenticationProvider provider = new OpenSaml4AuthenticationProvider();
        provider.setResponseAuthenticationConverter(token -> {
            Saml2Authentication base = OpenSaml4AuthenticationProvider
                .createDefaultResponseAuthenticationConverter().convert(token);
            Saml2AuthenticatedPrincipal principal =
                (Saml2AuthenticatedPrincipal) base.getPrincipal();
            List<String> groups = principal.getAttribute("groups");
            Collection<SimpleGrantedAuthority> authorities = groups == null ? List.of() :
                groups.stream()
                    .map(g -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + g.toUpperCase()))
                    .toList();
            return new Saml2Authentication(principal, base.getSaml2Response(), authorities);
        });
        return provider;
    }
}
```

**Endpoints that Spring Security exposes automatically:**
- **`GET /saml2/authenticate/{registrationId}`** — initiates SP-initiated SSO; redirects user to IdP SSO URL with `AuthnRequest`
- **`POST /login/saml2/sso/{registrationId}`** — ACS endpoint; receives `SAMLResponse`, validates, creates session
- **`POST /logout/saml2/slo`** — SP-initiated and IdP-initiated SLO
- **`GET /saml2/service-provider-metadata/{registrationId}`** — SP metadata XML for IdP onboarding

**Login link from your app:**

```html
<a href="/saml2/authenticate/okta">Login with Okta SSO</a>
```

**Custom claim/attribute mapping happens in `setResponseAuthenticationConverter`:**
- Default converter creates `Saml2Authentication` with empty authorities
- Override to read `groups`, `roles`, or any custom `AttributeStatement`
- Trust model: signature already validated by `OpenSaml4AuthenticationProvider` before converter runs
